  "nameStory": "The Frigid Mining Bee gets its chilly name from its remarkable ability to be active in cool weather when most other bees are still dormant. Unlike many bees that wait for warm spring days, this hardy species emerges early in the season and can forage even when temperatures are quite cold. The name 'frigid' reflects its cold-weather tolerance, making it one of the toughest bees in New England.",
  "description": "The Frigid Mining Bee is a small, solitary ground-nesting bee that's part of the large mining bee family. Like other mining bees, females dig individual burrows in soil to create underground nests for their young. These bees are typically dark-colored with some lighter markings and are about the size of a small honeybee. They're important early-season pollinators, visiting spring flowers when few other native bees are active.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cold-weather activity",
      "description": "Active on cool days when other bees remain hidden - look for them buzzing around flowers even when it feels too cold for most insects",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground nesting behavior",
      "description": "Females create small mounds of excavated soil around pencil-sized holes in bare or sparsely vegetated ground",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and build",
      "description": "About the size of a small honeybee with a compact, robust body built for cold-weather flying",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Early spring timing",
      "description": "Among the first native bees you'll see each year, often visiting early blooming trees and shrubs",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Solitary behavior",
      "description": "Works alone rather than in colonies - each female digs her own burrow and provisions it independently",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Frigid Mining Bees in areas with bare or lightly vegetated soil where females can easily excavate their burrows. They prefer well-drained sandy or loamy soils in sunny locations like gardens, trails, south-facing slopes, and woodland edges. You'll often find their small nesting holes in the same areas where spring wildflowers and early-blooming trees provide nectar sources nearby.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Peak activity time - adults emerge from underground and are busy mating, nest-building, and foraging on early flowers like willow, maple, and spring ephemerals. Look for small soil mounds around burrow entrances.",
    "summer": "Adult activity winds down by early summer as the current generation completes nesting. New adults may begin developing underground but won't emerge until the following spring.",
    "fall": "Adults are underground in their burrows, developing slowly through the cooler months. No surface activity to observe.",
    "winter": "Completely dormant underground in their natal burrows, waiting out the cold months until spring temperatures trigger emergence."
  },
  "funFact": "Frigid Mining Bees can fly and forage actively at temperatures as low as 45°F (7°C) - a feat that would leave most other bee species literally too cold to move their flight muscles!",
  "confusionNotes": "With over 60 mining bee species in New England, identifying specific Andrena species can be challenging and often requires close examination by experts. Focus on the cold-weather activity and early spring timing as the best field clues. Memory trick: 'Frigid flies first' - it's one of the earliest mining bees active each spring.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The name 'mining bee' comes from the females' behavior of excavating tunnels in the ground, much like miners digging shafts. Despite the name, these bees don't actually mine anything - they're just digging nurseries for their babies. All mining bees are solitary, meaning they don't form colonies like honeybees or bumblebees.",
  "compareHints": "Among the dozens of mining bee species in New England, Frigid Mining Bee stands out primarily for its exceptional cold tolerance and very early emergence timing. While many Andrena species look quite similar and require microscopic examination to distinguish, the Frigid Mining Bee's unique behavior of being active in cold weather when its relatives are dormant makes it more identifiable in the field. Other early-spring mining bees like the Spring Beauty Miner tend to wait for warmer days before becoming active.",
